          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="22.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#what-each-is-for" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Different Jobs</a>
          <a href="#performance" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Cleaning Performance</a>
          <a href="#effort" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. The Effort Question</a>
          <a href="#filtration-noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Filtration and Noise</a>
          <a href="#cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Five-Year Cost</a>
          <a href="#who-needs-which" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Which to Buy First</a>
          <a href="#getting-both-working" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Making Both Work</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Per pass, a conventional vacuum wins comfortably — more suction, a driven brush roll, and the ability to reach stairs and sofas. Over a week, the robot wins on hard floors, because it runs every day and the upright runs once. Most households need both, doing different jobs.
        </p>

        <div id="what-each-is-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. They Are Not Competing for the Same Job
          </h2>

          <p>
            The framing of robot versus upright is wrong, and it is why people are disappointed in both directions. A robot vacuum is not a replacement for a good vacuum cleaner; it is a replacement for the daily pass nobody makes.
          </p>

          <p>
            A conventional vacuum is a deep-cleaning tool used deliberately. It has a powerful motor, a driven brush roll, and — in a good one — sealed filtration, and once a week it removes embedded grit from carpet that nothing else will reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            A robot vacuum is a maintenance tool used continuously. It has a fraction of the suction and a much smaller brush, but it runs every day, which means dust and hair never accumulate in the first place. Its advantage is frequency, not capability.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once you see it that way, the answer for most households is obvious: run a robot daily and use a conventional vacuum every week or two for the deep clean. Neither does the other's job well, and together they cover the whole problem.
          </p>
          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Comparison That Actually Matters</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A daily light clean beats a weekly deep clean for how the floor looks and feels most of the time.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Dust and pet hair accumulate continuously. A robot that removes a little every day keeps the floor consistently presentable in a way a powerful vacuum used on Saturdays cannot, because the floor spends six days getting worse.</p>
          </div>

        </div>

        <div id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Cleaning Performance, Compared Honestly
          </h2>

          <p>
            On raw pickup per pass, a conventional vacuum wins and it is not close — particularly on carpet, where a driven brush roll agitates fibres in a way a small robot brush cannot match.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Conventional Vacuum</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Robot Vacuum</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hard floors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent — arguably better, daily</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Low-pile carpet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good with rubber rollers</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Medium and high-pile carpet</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fair — limited agitation and suction</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Edges and corners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good with a crevice tool</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fair — side brush helps, does not solve</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under furniture</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Poor — cannot reach</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Excellent, if it fits</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stairs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cannot do them at all</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Upholstery and car</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good with tools</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cannot do them at all</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Two rows in that table are the whole argument. A robot cannot do stairs, upholstery, or anything above floor level, which is why it cannot be your only vacuum in most homes. And a conventional vacuum cannot get under a sofa or a bed frame, which is precisely where dust accumulates undisturbed for years.
          </p>

          <p>
            On hard floors the comparison genuinely favours the robot, because the job there is picking up light surface dust and hair, and doing it daily beats doing it well. Households with hard floors throughout and a robot running every day often find the upright comes out monthly rather than weekly.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="effort" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Effort Question
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is where the robot earns its price, and it deserves an honest accounting because robots create small chores of their own.
          </p>

          <p>
            What disappears: the ten to twenty minutes a day of floor maintenance, the mental load of noticing that the floor needs doing, and the pet hair accumulating in corners between weekly cleans. In a house with a shedding animal this is genuinely transformative.
          </p>

          <p>
            What appears: a floor-tidying habit before each run if the robot lacks good obstacle avoidance, emptying a bin every few days unless you have a self-emptying dock, cleaning hair off the brush roll weekly, and occasionally rescuing a stuck machine.
          </p>

          <p>
            The net is strongly positive with a good machine and a self-emptying dock, and marginal with a cheap one that needs rescuing daily and has a bin you empty every second run. This is why obstacle avoidance and dock features matter more than suction figures — they decide whether the robot removes a chore or swaps it for another. Our <a href="/best-robot-vacuum/">robot vacuum comparison</a> ranks machines on exactly that basis.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="filtration-noise" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Filtration, Noise and Allergies
          </h2>

          <p>
            For anyone with allergies, the comparison shifts.
          </p>

          <p>
            A good conventional vacuum with genuinely sealed HEPA filtration captures fine dust and keeps it captured, and the best uprights and canisters are excellent at this. A robot vacuum's filtration is smaller and rarely sealed to the same standard, so a proportion of fine particles passes back into the room.
          </p>

          <p>
            But frequency cuts the other way again. A robot removing dust and dander daily means less of it accumulating to be disturbed in the first place, which for many allergy sufferers matters more than the filtration rating of a machine used weekly. The strongest combination is a robot for daily removal and a sealed HEPA vacuum for the weekly deep clean, with an air purifier handling what becomes airborne — our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-allergies/">allergy purifier guide</a> covers that side.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise favours the robot clearly: 55 to 65 dB against 70 to 80 dB for most uprights, and the robot's noise happens while you are out rather than while you are trying to hear the television. The exception is the self-emptying dock, which is briefly very loud — 75 to 85 dB for fifteen seconds — and worth thinking about before siting one outside a bedroom door.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            There is also a practical difference in how each machine handles a house with several people in it. A conventional vacuum requires somebody to decide to use it, which in most households means one person does nearly all of it. A robot on a schedule removes that negotiation entirely, and for a surprising number of families that is the part they value most — not the cleaning, but no longer having a recurring conversation about whose turn it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            The counterpart is that a robot needs the floor to be tidy enough to run. In a house where floors collect shoes, toys and charging cables, somebody still has to do a five-minute pass before the robot goes out, and if nobody does, the robot spends its run stuck against a sock. Good obstacle avoidance reduces that burden a great deal but does not remove it.
          </p>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Cost of Ownership Over Five Years
          </h2>

          <p>
            Purchase price is only part of it, and the two categories diverge in interesting ways.
          </p>
          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Conventional Vacuum</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Robot Vacuum</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Purchase</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$120-$900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$250-$1,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Consumables per year</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$30-$80</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$100-$200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery replacement</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$50-$120 every 3-5 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Typical lifespan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-10 years</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-7 years</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repairability</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Good — belts, brushes, filters</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Moderate — brushes, battery, sensors</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Robot consumables are higher than people expect: dock bags, filters, side brushes, main brushes and mop pads all wear, and a busy house spends $100 to $200 a year. Batteries are the other recurring cost, typically needing replacement at the three to five year mark.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conventional vacuums are cheaper to run and generally last longer, largely because there is less to fail and what does fail — a belt, a brush roll, a filter — is easy and cheap to replace.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither observation changes the recommendation. The robot is not competing on cost per year; it is buying back time every single day, and that is the correct frame for judging it.
          </p>

        </div>

        <div id="who-needs-which" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Which One Should You Buy First?
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you own neither, buy the conventional vacuum first. It is the machine that can do everything — stairs, upholstery, deep carpet, the car — and no home is properly equipped without one.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you own a conventional vacuum and are considering a robot, the value depends on your floors and your household. Hard floors, pets, children, or anyone who resents daily tidying: a robot is one of the better appliance purchases available. Thick carpet throughout, a very cluttered floor plan, or a house on several levels with no lift: the value drops sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are considering replacing a dying upright with a robot instead, think carefully. Stairs are the usual dealbreaker, followed by the car and the sofa. A cordless stick vacuum plus a robot is a better answer than a robot alone — the stick handles stairs, upholstery and spot cleaning, and the robot handles the floors.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a small apartment with hard floors, a robot plus a handheld genuinely can be the whole vacuum inventory, and many people live that way happily.
          </p>

        </div>

          <p>
            One more consideration for multi-storey homes: a robot maps and cleans one floor at a time, and carrying it up and down stairs defeats much of the convenience. Some machines store several floor maps and cope well with being moved, but the dock stays where it is, so the upstairs run happens without self-emptying. Households that value the robot most usually end up with one per floor, or accept that it covers the main living level only.
          </p>

        <div id="getting-both-working" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Making the Two Work Together
          </h2>

          <p>
            Households that get the most out of both settle into a simple division of labour.
          </p>

          <p>
            The robot runs daily, usually while the house is empty, covering all accessible floor area. That keeps surface dust and hair permanently down and means the floor always looks recently cleaned.
          </p>

          <p>
            The conventional vacuum comes out every week or two for carpet, stairs, edges, upholstery and the places the robot cannot reach. Because the robot has been removing the surface layer daily, this deep clean takes less time and produces less dust in the air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits make the arrangement work. Keep floors tidy enough that the robot can run unattended — a five-minute pass picking up cables and socks each evening — and set the robot to run on a schedule rather than manually, because a robot you have to remember to start is a robot that runs twice a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not expect the robot to handle a mess. It is a maintenance machine: excellent at the steady accumulation of daily dust and hair, poor at a spilled bag of flour or a broken plant pot. That is what the conventional vacuum — or, for anything genuinely messy, a <a href="/best-shop-vac/">shop vac</a> — is for.
          </p>

        </div>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Robot to Replace a Deep Clean</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A robot removes the surface layer daily; it does not lift embedded grit from carpet, and it cannot reach stairs, upholstery or car interiors. Households that bin the upright after buying a robot usually buy another vacuum within a year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Robot for a Thickly Carpeted House</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Medium and high-pile carpet defeats most robots: limited suction, small brushes, and sometimes difficulty driving on the pile at all. The value is far higher on hard floors and low pile, and no amount of Pascal rating changes the physics of agitation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging a Robot on Suction Figures</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Above roughly 5,000 Pa the difference on ordinary floors is hard to detect, while obstacle avoidance and dock features decide whether the machine runs unattended every day. Buy for autonomy — that is the feature that turns a robot from a gadget into a time saving.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Siting a Self-Emptying Dock Outside a Bedroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The dock is briefly very loud, typically 75 to 85 dB for fifteen seconds at the end of each run. It is easy to forget when choosing a spot, and it is the most common reason people move the dock a week after installing it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running a Robot Manually Instead of on a Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A robot you have to remember to start runs twice a week and delivers a fraction of its value. Schedule it daily while the house is empty; the entire benefit comes from frequency, and frequency only happens automatically.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a robot vacuum replace a regular vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In a small apartment with hard floors, often yes — paired with a handheld for stairs and upholstery. In most homes, no. A robot cannot do stairs, sofas, car interiors or anything above floor level, and it cannot match a driven brush roll on medium or high-pile carpet. Treat it as a daily maintenance machine, not a replacement.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a robot vacuum as good as a regular vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Per pass, no — a conventional vacuum has far more suction and a powered brush roll that agitates carpet fibres. Over a week, the comparison changes, because the robot runs daily and the upright runs once. On hard floors, daily beats deep for how the floor actually looks and feels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do robot vacuums work on carpet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On low-pile carpet, yes, particularly models with rubber rollers. On medium and high-pile carpet they struggle: limited suction and small brushes cannot lift embedded grit, and some robots have trouble driving on thick pile at all. Carpeted homes still need a conventional vacuum for the deep clean.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a robot vacuum worth it if I already have a good vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you have hard floors, pets or children, yes — it removes the daily maintenance pass, which is the chore people actually resent. If you have thick carpet throughout, a very cluttered floor plan, or multiple levels, the value drops considerably because the robot covers less of the work.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are robot vacuums good for allergies?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They help, mostly through frequency rather than filtration — daily removal means less dust and dander accumulating to be disturbed. Their filtration is generally less well sealed than a good HEPA upright, so the strongest arrangement is a robot for daily pickup, a sealed HEPA vacuum weekly, and an air purifier in the bedroom.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is cheaper to own, a robot or a regular vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A conventional vacuum, clearly. Robot consumables run $100 to $200 a year in a busy house once you count dock bags, filters, brushes and mop pads, plus a battery every three to five years. Conventional vacuums cost $30 to $80 a year and typically last longer. The robot is buying time, not saving money.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a robot vacuum or a cordless stick vacuum?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They solve different problems. A stick vacuum is for spot cleaning, stairs, upholstery and quick messes; a robot is for keeping the whole floor consistently clean without you. If you can only have one alongside an existing upright, choose by whether your frustration is daily floor maintenance or awkward spot cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

